      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "RATIONALE",
          "content": "Aorto-esophageal fistula (AEF), a postoperative complication of esophagectomy, constitutes a very small percentage of all cases presenting with AEF; however, it is associated with a high mortality rate. Acute massive hemorrhage is the single largest cause of death in patients developing AEF. There is a lack of consensus on the optimal treatment of AEF."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "PATIENT CONCERNS",
          "content": "We present 3 cases secondary to esophagectomy due to lower thoracic esophageal carcinoma. All 3 patients presented with similar acute symptoms including a critical and life-threatening course with dead feeling, thoracic pain, and projectile hematemesis, and also hypovolemic shock."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "DIAGNOSES",
          "content": "Digital subtraction angiography identified AEF as the diagnosis of these 3 cases."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTIONS",
          "content": "All patients were treated with emergency thoracic endovascular aortic repair, the aortic fistulas were repaired."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "OUTCOMES",
          "content": "All patients successfully survived the perioperative period."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "LESSONS",
          "content": "Thoracic endovascular aortic repair, a minimally invasive technique, is a better method to achieve faster and safer hemodynamic stability in patients with AEF compared with open thoracic aortic repair."
        }
      ]},
